#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054276 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "228b0a63-bf0e-57bf-aa1e-ef5518236462" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Mathematical Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "db1f7851-43e9-5fee-9553-f9d6ead38b40" :question/math-academy-id "e-12675" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P$: Compute the product of the first four prime numbers.\n- $Q$: Four is a prime number.\n- $R$: Is four a prime number?" :question/worked-solution "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false. Rather, it is an instruction.\n- $Q$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because $4$ is not a prime number (it is divisible by $2$).\n- $R$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false. Rather, it is a question, and the possible answers are \"yes\" or \"no.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054277 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f185774-4d55-515c-9032-8f6832c6999a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Truth Value of a Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "8ca6e60c-1411-5f6e-a9f4-d6209f847ddf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12676" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P$: $4$ is a positive number.\n- $Q$: $2^3$ is a natural number.\n- $R$: $-4 \\geq 2$" :question/worked-solution "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate the truth value of each statement:\n\n- Statement $P$ is *true.* Indeed, $4$ is a positive number.\n- Statement $Q$ is *true.* Indeed, $2^3=8$ is a natural number.\n- Statement $R$ is *false* because $-4 \\ngeq 2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054280 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eda1965-d655-5550-9bb5-13e7a9c1b406" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statements Expressed Using Symbols"}] :question/id #uuid "d85fd26e-50f6-58f1-9e44-8df23863ee6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12685" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P: \\{5,6,7\\}\\cap \\{6,8,10\\} = \\{6\\}$\n- $Q: \\sqrt{3} +2$\n- $R: 4\\mid 2$\n\n**Note**: The notation\n$a\\mid b$\nmeans \"$a$ divides $b$.\" This is equivalent to the statement that $b$ is divisible by $a$." :question/worked-solution "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because the intersection of the given sets is indeed the set $\\{6\\}$.\n- $Q$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because $4$ does not divide $2$ (i.e., $2$ is not divisible by $4$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054364 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35eba041-5109-5855-b132-174c0271c168" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates"}] :question/id #uuid "a70765b4-08b9-5ebf-92f4-1b7c5266523e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13288" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n1. Subtract $x$ from $10$.\n2. $y^2 -2 \\leq 3$\n3. $1+z$ is a perfect square." :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options.\n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it gives an instruction.\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eda1965-d655-5550-9bb5-13e7a9c1b406" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statements Expressed Using Symbols"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da6cd1ac-9cee-5e7a-a79d-d10fa308412b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051648} {:answer/id #uuid "efe7d6d0-dc3f-53bb-a914-21697d688358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051649} {:answer/id #uuid "f04b72f7-fe4b-5129-8f07-7e6894b49a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051650} {:answer/id #uuid "ff48860c-8fa9-59e1-acde-fc57509581c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051651} {:answer/id #uuid "312073aa-646c-5cec-83b4-0b58601e7e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efe7d6d0-dc3f-53bb-a914-21697d688358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051649} :answer-field/id #uuid "208ea793-e173-57b3-828f-fcef5a185ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051647}] :question/id #uuid "8a13c06f-6317-5f54-a60c-2244cd05e4c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-170843" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P: 2 \\mid 4$\n- $Q: \\sqrt{2} ∈ N$\n- $R: 2 + 2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eda1965-d655-5550-9bb5-13e7a9c1b406" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statements Expressed Using Symbols"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a0f0e7-fa95-5e04-858d-7ea18bd2816d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051654} {:answer/id #uuid "adfdb015-c086-501d-abae-1f128533be81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051655} {:answer/id #uuid "b8707c48-7841-5867-9287-d70b41f30c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051656} {:answer/id #uuid "348c190b-7c67-5c29-8409-f15d6b95eb28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051657} {:answer/id #uuid "39f9af3a-8a7f-56fe-a4d0-b1e82fbbb6f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84a0f0e7-fa95-5e04-858d-7ea18bd2816d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051654} :answer-field/id #uuid "750758cb-b066-537b-acd0-366694517569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051653}] :question/id #uuid "d26247f5-ea0b-58f7-ab8c-9e2873f56e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-170844" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P: {1, 2, 3}∩{4, 5, 6} = ∅$\n- $Q: \\sqrt{3^{2} - 4^{2}}$\n- $R: 4$ is a prime number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35eba041-5109-5855-b132-174c0271c168" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b6237d-6466-5